    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Medical services in Iran span outpatient consultations, hospital treatment, diagnostic testing, rehabilitation, and care outside hospitals. Public, private, charitable, and employment-linked providers participate, with important differences in access and financing.",
    "tags": ["Medical services", "Outpatient care", "Diagnostics", "Rehabilitation", "Health financing"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:00:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:00:16Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Medical Services in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An introduction to Iran’s consultation, treatment, diagnostic, and follow-up services, including provider differences and evidence gaps."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "service-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A network of different services",
        "content": "Iran’s medical-service landscape extends beyond hospitals. WHO’s assessment of the private sector describes clinical care delivered through hospitals, clinics, polyclinics, and independent practices. Its clinical-pathway figure also includes diagnostic and treatment transitions, rehabilitation, nursing, and home care. A polyclinic brings multiple consultation services into one setting; an independent practice is a separate practitioner’s service. These distinctions help explain why a patient’s care may involve several organizations rather than one institution. [s1](https://www.ccpsh.org/sites/default/files/uploaded_files/2025-10/PSE%2520in%2520I.R.%2520Iran-eng.pdf)",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ownership-financing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Who provides care, and who pays?",
        "content": "Provider ownership and payment arrangements are separate questions. Alongside public and private providers, the Social Security Organization operates facilities and purchases services. It is therefore both a financing institution and a participant in service delivery. WHO’s assessment also identifies private and charitable surgical centres within outpatient care. A service’s location outside a public hospital does not, by itself, reveal its insurance arrangements or the patient’s eventual bill. [s1]\n\nA policy analysis published in 2021 found that insurance expansion had improved coverage while leaving significant financial-protection problems. In particular, the gap between public and private tariffs exposed patients using private services to additional expenditure. This is a historical finding about financing mechanisms, not a quotation of current charges or a statement that every insured patient faces the same payment. [s2](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12939-020-01372-4)",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "service-branches",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding the branches",
        "content": "This directory separates services by their main function. Clinics concern consultations and outpatient treatment; surgical centres concern procedures; laboratories and imaging centres concern different forms of diagnostic investigation. Rehabilitation and home care extend the view beyond the initial consultation or hospital episode. WHO’s pathway framework supports reading these services as connected parts of care, while leaving room for different provider arrangements. The categories are navigation aids rather than a verified legal classification of facilities. [s1]\n\nThe distinction matters when assessing capacity. A count of consultation sites cannot describe the availability of imaging, rehabilitation, or follow-up. Nor does an insurance enrolment figure establish that every service is locally available or financially protected. The financing analysis emphasizes that population coverage, benefits, and protection against costs are separate dimensions of universal health coverage. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "geography-and-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Availability differs across places",
        "content": "The Commonwealth Fund’s Iran profile describes an important private-sector role in urban specialist care and regional disparities in specialist distribution. It also identifies financial and nonfinancial barriers affecting access, including geography and physical accessibility. Consequently, a national description of available specialties should not be interpreted as evidence that the same services can be reached equally easily from every community. [s3](https://www.commonwealthfund.org/international-health-policy-center/countries/iran)\n\nFor international readers, this makes local context essential to understanding the service system. The relevant questions include the provider’s location, the kind of care offered, its relationship to other services, and the financing arrangements described by the applicable institution. This page explains the system rather than recommending individual providers or establishing eligibility for treatment. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assessing-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Quality includes the experience of care",
        "content": "Service quality also concerns what happens around the clinical encounter. A systematic review of Iranian hospital outpatient services identified responsiveness as a particular weakness and discussed timely attention, courteous interaction, and attention to patients’ needs. Its findings concern the studies it combined, rather than a current national rating of all medical services. [s4](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-023-09506-4)\n\nThe useful starting point is therefore a connected view: consultations, investigations, treatment, and follow-up must be understood together with affordability and access. The available research supports that framework, but a fuller account still requires service-specific evidence on rehabilitation, home care, diagnostics, and contemporary operating conditions.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
