    "description": "Clinics form part of Iran’s outpatient service network alongside hospital departments and independent practices. Understanding their role requires distinguishing the services offered, the organization operating them, and the arrangements used to pay for care.",
    "tags": ["Clinics", "Outpatient services", "Patient experience", "Insurance", "Service quality"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:00:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:00:16Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Clinics and Outpatient Care in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand clinics within Iran’s outpatient network, including ownership, financial protection, patient experience, and research limitations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "clinic-settings",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Several settings for outpatient care",
        "content": "A clinic is one setting within a wider outpatient network. WHO’s Iran assessment lists clinics and polyclinics alongside hospital services and independent practitioners. These facilities operate across public, private, and Social Security Organization arrangements. A clinic should therefore not automatically be understood as privately owned, and an outpatient consultation does not necessarily take place in a stand-alone building. [s1](https://www.ccpsh.org/sites/default/files/uploaded_files/2025-10/PSE%2520in%2520I.R.%2520Iran-eng.pdf)\n\nThe Social Security Organization, an employment-linked social insurance institution, has its own clinics and polyclinics as well as arrangements to purchase care. This illustrates why ownership and financing need separate explanations: the organization delivering a consultation may also operate an insurance scheme, or it may receive payment through a contract with another institution. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "payment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Insurance does not settle every cost",
        "content": "Clinic access has an important financial dimension. A 2021 policy analysis identified the difference between public and private tariffs as a source of out-of-pocket expenditure, particularly because private providers have a substantial outpatient role. Its discussion shows why holding insurance and receiving full financial protection are different matters. [s2](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12939-020-01372-4)\n\nThose findings describe the period examined by the researchers. They do not establish a current copayment, a universal clinic fee, or the benefits available under a particular policy. For explanatory purposes, the key distinction is between the provider’s charge and the amount an insurer recognizes or reimburses. A complete contemporary account would need verified tariff schedules and contract terms rather than a single national price. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "patient-experience",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What research can establish about quality",
        "content": "Research on hospital outpatient services offers evidence about the consultation experience, but its scope needs care. A systematic review published in 2023 compared patients’ perceptions with their expectations and identified responsiveness as a weak dimension. The authors emphasized prompt service, courteous interaction, and attention to patients’ needs. These findings concern patient-reported service experience; they are not a direct measurement of diagnostic accuracy or treatment safety. [s3](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-023-09506-4)\n\nThe review also reported substantial differences between the studies it combined, related to their populations and settings. Its results should therefore not be turned into a league table of Iranian clinics or a rating applied to an individual facility. Hospital outpatient evidence cannot automatically stand for independent practices or every kind of community clinic. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding a clinic’s place in care",
        "content": "The most useful account of a clinic identifies what consultations it provides, who operates it, and how its patients connect with investigations or further treatment. WHO’s service-pathway framework places outpatient care within a broader sequence that includes hospitals and follow-up. This makes continuity an important part of understanding the setting, alongside the consultation itself. [s1]\n\nThe evidence supports a mixed outpatient landscape rather than one standardized clinic model. More detailed coverage requires a verified contemporary distinction between facility types and their permitted services. Until that evidence is available, this page remains an introduction rather than a directory or a comprehensive guide to clinic regulation.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
